467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we pull out the factor of $2$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{2(x+\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n|  | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $5$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n|  | $5$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ |\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-1}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})} & =5-\\frac{(\\frac{7}{2})}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =5-\\frac{7}{2(x+\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =5-\\frac{7}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(5-\\frac{7}{2x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[5{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x-7{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[5x{|}_{0}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|{|}_{0}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[5(2-0)-\\frac{7}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2\\cdot 2+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2\\cdot 0+1|)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[10-\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5] \\\\  & =5-\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042267/state.json" "file_sha256" "76d9a54e523855792e179a9597805116dd945a177b4ed6b03a30ef36d5996700"} {"question" "q-49467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042267/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d8e91c02ba20fbea134c908e328151703f7c74397295a2b0d8d666d753e26a5"} {"question" "q-49467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042267/state.json" "file_sha256" "76d9a54e523855792e179a9597805116dd945a177b4ed6b03a30ef36d5996700"} {"question" "q-49467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "2+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡7"] ["math" "5-\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042267/state.json" "file_sha256" "76d9a54e523855792e179a9597805116dd945a177b4ed6b03a30ef36d5996700"} {"question" "q-49467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5-\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042267/state.json" "file_sha256" "76d9a54e523855792e179a9597805116dd945a177b4ed6b03a30ef36d5996700"} {"question" "e-485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 \\dfrac{4x-1}{x+1}\\,\\textrm d x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042267/example-485.json" "file_sha256" "ef188c4641dbce7d6568c40e3f7f7b217143010192f8f5b32fe89235efa21dcf"} {"question" "e-485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The denominator of the integrand doesn't divide the numerator exactly, so we need to decompose it into its quotient and remainder. For this, we can use synthetic division.\n\nWe start by drawing up our synthetic division table:\n\n|  | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-1$ | $4$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $4$ | $-5$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4x-1}{x+1} = 4 -\\dfrac{5}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4x-1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4-\\frac{5}{x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\,\\text{d}x-5{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4x{|}_{0}^{1}-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[4-0]-5[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1] \\\\  & =4-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042267/example-485.json" "file_sha256" "ef188c4641dbce7d6568c40e3f7f7b217143010192f8f5b32fe89235efa21dcf"} {"question" "e-486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2x^2-3x-4}{x-3}\\,\\textrm d x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042267/example-486.json" "file_sha256" "f3bd8b3c13b9c88abdc940a44368c9addd9ad1b2eac275b00090df5f1d4c70df"} {"question" "e-486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division. \n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n|  | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}3$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n|  | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{ 2x^2-3x-4}{x-3} = 2x + 3 + \\dfrac {5}{x-3} .\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-4}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+3+\\frac{5}{x-3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+3x+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042267/example-486.json" "file_sha256" "f3bd8b3c13b9c88abdc940a44368c9addd9ad1b2eac275b00090df5f1d4c70df"} {"question" "e-487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{x-1}{2x-1}\\,\\textrm d x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042267/example-487.json" "file_sha256" "74cc433dc6e312bb5f45b15cb080b8b7ff7c6fb7a9326388395de2dc44daf0bc"} {"question" "e-487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we pull out the factor of $2$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x-1}{2(x-\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n|  | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $\\dfrac12$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})} & =1-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2(x-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (1-\\frac{1}{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042267/example-487.json" "file_sha256" "74cc433dc6e312bb5f45b15cb080b8b7ff7c6fb7a9326388395de2dc44daf0bc"}] "usage" {"e-486" [] "e-485" [] "e-487" [] "q-49467" []} "basis" 1539 "retractions" [[17592186064784 :question/problem "Evaluate $∫_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x - 1}{2x + 1}dx$."] [17592186064009 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 \\dfrac{4x-1}{x+1}\\,\\textrm d x$."] [17592186064009 :question/worked-solution "The denominator of the integrand doesn't divide the numerator exactly, so we need to decompose it into its quotient and remainder. For this, we can use synthetic division.\n\nWe start by drawing up our synthetic division table:\n\n|  | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}-1$ | $4$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $4$ | $-5$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4x-1}{x+1} = 4 -\\dfrac{5}{x+1}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}\\frac{4x - 1}{x + 1}dx &= ∫_{0}^{1}(4 - \\frac{5}{x + 1})dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}4dx - 5∫_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x + 1}dx \\\\\n&= 4x\\begin{vmatrix}_{0}^{1} - 5\\ln & x + 1\\end{vmatrix} \\mid _{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= [4 - 0] - 5[\\ln 2 - \\ln 1] \\\\\n&= 4 - 5\\ln 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186064010 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2x^2-3x-4}{x-3}\\,\\textrm d x$."] [17592186064010 :question/worked-solution "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division.\n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n|  | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}3$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n|  | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{2x^2-3x-4}{x-3} = 2x + 3 + \\dfrac {5}{x-3}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{2x^{2} - 3x - 4}{x - 3}dx &= ∫(2x + 3 + \\frac{5}{x - 3})dx \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 3x + 5\\ln \\mid x - 3 \\mid + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186064011 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{x-1}{2x-1}\\,\\textrm d x$."] [17592186064011 :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $2$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{x - 1}{2x - 1}dx &= ∫(x - 1)/(2(x - \\frac{1}{2}))dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫(x - 1)/((x - \\frac{1}{2}))dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n|  | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $\\dfrac12$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 1)/((x - \\frac{1}{2})) &= 1 - ((\\frac{1}{2}))/((x - \\frac{1}{2})) \\\\\n&= 1 - (1)/(2(x - \\frac{1}{2})) \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{1}{2x - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{x - 1}{2x - 1}dx &= \\frac{1}{2}∫(1 - \\frac{1}{2x - 1})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}[x - \\frac{1}{2}\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}2x - 1 & ] + C \\\\ = \\frac{1}{2}x - \\frac{1}{4}\\ln & 2x - 1\\end{vmatrix}+ C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186063215 17592186063216 17592186063217 17592186063218 17592186063219] "immutable_fields" [[17592186063214 :answer-field/correct] [17592186063214 :answer-field/id] [17592186063214 :answer-field/key] [17592186063214 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
